= First-order logic =

== Overview ==
'''First-order logic''' extends propositional logic with terms, predicates, variables, relations, and quantifiers ranging over individuals in a domain.

It is a central formal language in mathematics, philosophy, computer science, and knowledge representation.

== Predicates and terms ==
Predicates express properties or relations:

<pre>
Human(x)
Loves(x,y)
</pre>

Terms denote or range over objects.

== Quantifiers ==
First-order logic uses quantifiers such as:

<pre>
∀x P(x)   — for every x, P(x)
∃x P(x)   — for some x, P(x)
</pre>

Quantifiers can be nested to represent complex relational structure.

== First-order restriction ==
First-order quantifiers range over individuals in the domain.

They do not directly quantify over predicates, properties, or sets of individuals.

That distinguishes first-order logic from second-order and higher-order systems.

== Semantics ==
A first-order model supplies:

* a domain of objects;
* interpretations for constants;
* functions;
* predicates and relations.

Truth is evaluated relative to a model and variable assignment.

== Metalogical properties ==
Classical first-order logic has major properties including:

* soundness;
* Gödel completeness;
* compactness;
* Löwenheim–Skolem theorems.

Validity is not decidable in general, although it is recursively enumerable.

== Naming ==
“Predicate logic” and “predicate calculus” are common historical or informal terms.

The canonical BITwiki title is '''First-order logic''' because “predicate logic” can be used more broadly and does not cleanly mark the first-order boundary.

